          "heading": "Case study: Irrigated floodplain soil profile",
          "content": "## Case study: Irrigated floodplain soil profile\n\nA field experiment measures water entry into loamy soil under ponding. Conductivity and wetting-front suction produce a plausible Green\u2013Ampt response, but the experimental surface develops a sediment seal midway through the test. A single constant-parameter fit may then reproduce total infiltration while misrepresenting the rate history.\n\n**Investigation task.** Estimate soil wetness and test the fit against early and late observations separately; state why ET0 does not measure infiltration.\n\n### The unit as part of a watershed investigation\n\nThis unit asks an engineer to defend infiltration and et as a quantitative argument rather than a number inserted into a generic formula. The natural control volume is the selected hydrologic subsystem and its actual inflow and outflow boundaries. Write the unit of every parameter and specify the temporal support of the observations; an hourly intensity, a daily depth and an annual volume cannot be substituted for each other without integration. A concept sketch should show the site boundaries, measurement stations, surface and subsurface pathways, relevant human infrastructure and the time interval over which the process operates. Interpret the final result relative to the real decision at irrigated floodplain soil profile, not merely against an arbitrary calculator threshold.\n\n### Field evidence, scale and uncertainty\n\nThe most important practical complication in evapotranspiration and infiltration is that model inputs are estimates of spatially and temporally varying states. Two instruments reporting the same variable may not have comparable averaging periods or calibration. The impact of an uncertain coefficient depends on the derivative of the output with respect to that coefficient and on where the operating point falls relative to a threshold. If a predicted response appears robust, deliberately test a neighboring process that the simple model omits: channel blockage, antecedent saturation, high tailwater, sensor drift, groundwater exchange or a different storm shape. Record failures of the model openly, rather than editing the inputs until a visually pleasing curve appears.\n\n### Professional inference standard\n\nAn educational simulation is useful when it generates a falsifiable prediction. Before using any of the four lessons to make a design claim, identify an independently measurable output, a second approximation with different assumptions and a failure consequence. For irrigated floodplain soil profile, a field campaign might require time-synchronized precipitation and flow observations, a georeferenced inventory of hydraulic structures, separate dry- and wet-weather site visits and a provenance ledger for every parameter. The exercises below are intentionally synthetic and no parameter set is asserted to describe a real Bangladesh, US or other watershed. Local codes and agency-approved hydrologic methods supersede these teaching screens."
