          "content": "## Applied project 3 \u2014 Irrigated floodplain soil profile\n\nA field experiment measures water entry into loamy soil under ponding. Conductivity and wetting-front suction produce a plausible Green\u2013Ampt response, but the experimental surface develops a sediment seal midway through the test. A single constant-parameter fit may then reproduce total infiltration while misrepresenting the rate history.\n\n**Project deliverable.** Estimate soil wetness and test the fit against early and late observations separately; state why ET0 does not measure infiltration. Include an explicit data dictionary, local measurement geometry, at least two competing model structures, a water-balance closure or proxy and a sensitivity analysis. Use the two paired laboratories for this unit as exploratory tools only. A peer should be able to reproduce the results using the included synthetic inputs and code without requesting undocumented assumptions.\n\n### Unit review and source-of-error audit\n\nBefore claiming success, ask whether the result is constrained by water conservation, the measurement period, the sampling interval and the domain of the empirical relation. Identify how a missing observation could change a decision, not merely how it changes the last decimal digit. The strongest student reports explain *why* a model is unsuitable for a particular site, include a second defensible interpretation and propose a feasible experiment to choose between them."
