          "content": "## Applied project 4 \u2014 Hill-foot settlement runoff response\n\nA 7.5 km\u00b2 catchment contains steep cultivated land, paved pathways and riparian vegetation. For a storm with 75 mm rainfall, an assumed CN of 78 gives S about 71.6 mm, Ia about 14.3 mm and event excess roughly 28.0 mm. This is a scenario, not a measured event. A short intense burst may concentrate runoff before all hillslopes become connected.\n\n**Project deliverable.** Calculate scenario runoff volume; compare a CN change to 85, then draw a triangular hydrograph that conserves the resulting water volume. Include an explicit data dictionary, local measurement geometry, at least two competing model structures, a water-balance closure or proxy and a sensitivity analysis. Use the two paired laboratories for this unit as exploratory tools only. A peer should be able to reproduce the results using the included synthetic inputs and code without requesting undocumented assumptions.\n\n### Unit review and source-of-error audit\n\nBefore claiming success, ask whether the result is constrained by water conservation, the measurement period, the sampling interval and the domain of the empirical relation. Identify how a missing observation could change a decision, not merely how it changes the last decimal digit. The strongest student reports explain *why* a model is unsuitable for a particular site, include a second defensible interpretation and propose a feasible experiment to choose between them."
