          "content": "## Applied project 5 \u2014 Wetland flood peak attenuation\n\nA freshwater wetland receiving a constant 50 m\u00b3/s from an upstream channel initially releases 10 m\u00b3/s. A linear storage timescale of 5 hours predicts an increasing outflow but no immediate match with inflow. Downstream bridge operations depend on the peak time rather than just the total event volume.\n\n**Project deliverable.** Calculate outflow at 1, 3 and 6 hours and explain why an actual wetland stage-discharge function may not be linear. Include an explicit data dictionary, local measurement geometry, at least two competing model structures, a water-balance closure or proxy and a sensitivity analysis. Use the two paired laboratories for this unit as exploratory tools only. A peer should be able to reproduce the results using the included synthetic inputs and code without requesting undocumented assumptions.\n\n### Unit review and source-of-error audit\n\nBefore claiming success, ask whether the result is constrained by water conservation, the measurement period, the sampling interval and the domain of the empirical relation. Identify how a missing observation could change a decision, not merely how it changes the last decimal digit. The strongest student reports explain *why* a model is unsuitable for a particular site, include a second defensible interpretation and propose a feasible experiment to choose between them."
