          "content": "## Applied project 8 \u2014 Hydrological model review board\n\nA model calibrated on one intense storm appears to reproduce the observed peak, but the discharge integral is biased upward and low-flow periods are systematically overpredicted. A high NSE on a few peak-dominated values does not ensure the model is suitable for monthly drought allocation. Different stakeholders will use different parts of the hydrograph.\n\n**Project deliverable.** Create a validation dashboard with volume ratio, peak magnitude, peak time, NSE and uncertainty, and select a safe first decision. Include an explicit data dictionary, local measurement geometry, at least two competing model structures, a water-balance closure or proxy and a sensitivity analysis. Use the two paired laboratories for this unit as exploratory tools only. A peer should be able to reproduce the results using the included synthetic inputs and code without requesting undocumented assumptions.\n\n### Unit review and source-of-error audit\n\nBefore claiming success, ask whether the result is constrained by water conservation, the measurement period, the sampling interval and the domain of the empirical relation. Identify how a missing observation could change a decision, not merely how it changes the last decimal digit. The strongest student reports explain *why* a model is unsuitable for a particular site, include a second defensible interpretation and propose a feasible experiment to choose between them."
